DATASET:
Name: Impact of Social Media on Life (KAGGLE)
Link: https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/harishyadav0506/impact-of-social-media-on-life
Description: This dataset represents the impact of social media on life and how digital engagement shapes daily life and well being, and it has records from 4,500 students ranging from high school to postgraduate studies.


DATA PREPERATION AND STANDARIZATION:
Features: Age, Daily_Usage_Hours, Sleep_Duration_Hours
Why I chose these feautures: These features are relevant numeric indicators for analyzing a students lifestyle behaviors, routines, and physical habits.
Why Standardization is important: Models like K Means rely on distance measurements. So without scaling, features with larger numeric ranges would jsut dominate the distance calculation and mess up the results. Standardization changes the features to have a mean of 0 and a standard deviation of 1, which makes sure every feature contributes equally.


K-MEANS, HIERARCHICHAL CLUSTERING, AND PCA MODELS:

from google.colab import drive
drive.mount('/content/drive')

import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

from sklearn.cluster import KMeans
from sklearn.cluster import AgglomerativeClustering
from sklearn.decomposition import PCA
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler


#Getting the Dataset 
file_path = "/content/drive/MyDrive/AI100/SocialMedia.csv"

df = pd.read_csv(file_path)

print("Social Media Users")
print(df)


#Features used for clustering
X = df[["Age", "Daily_Usage_Hours", "Sleep_Duration_Hours"]]


#Standardizing the data
scaler = StandardScaler()

X_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X)


#K-MEANS CLUSTERING
kmeans = KMeans(n_clusters=3, random_state=42, n_init=10)

df["KMeans_Cluster"] = kmeans.fit_predict(X_scaled)

print("\nK-Means Results:")
print(df)


#HIERARCHICAL CLUSTERING
hierarchical = AgglomerativeClustering(n_clusters=3) 

df["Hierarchical_Cluster"] = hierarchical.fit_predict(X_scaled)

print("\nHierarchical Clustering Results:")
print(df)


#PCA
pca = PCA(n_components=2) 

X_pca = pca.fit_transform(X_scaled)
 
df["PC1"] = X_pca[:, 0]
df["PC2"] = X_pca[:, 1]

print("\nPCA Results:")
print(df[["Age", "Daily_Usage_Hours", "Sleep_Duration_Hours", "PC1", "PC2"]])

print("\nExplained Variance:")
print(pca.explained_variance_ratio_)

 
#Visualizing K-Means
plt.scatter(
    df["Daily_Usage_Hours"],
    df["Sleep_Duration_Hours"],
    c=df["KMeans_Cluster"]
)

plt.xlabel("Daily Usage Hours")
plt.ylabel("Sleep Duration Hours")
plt.title("K-Means Clustering")
plt.show()


#Visualizing Hierarchical Clustering
plt.scatter(
    df["Daily_Usage_Hours"],
    df["Sleep_Duration_Hours"],
    c=df["Hierarchical_Cluster"]
)

plt.xlabel("Daily Usage Hours")
plt.ylabel("Sleep Duration Hours")
plt.title("Hierarchical Clustering")
plt.show()


#Visualizing PCA
plt.scatter(
    df["PC1"],
    df["PC2"],
    c=df["KMeans_Cluster"]
)

plt.xlabel("Principal Component 1")
plt.ylabel("Principal Component 2")
plt.title("PCA")
plt.show()


RESULTS AND INTERPRETATION:

K-Means Clustering Results: K-Means successfully separated the students into distinct behavioral groups based on daily screen time versus hours slept. 
Overall Conclusion: The visualization clearly shows an inverse relationship—the more hours a student spent on social media, the fewer hours they slept. However, the boundaries between normal and low social media usage groups appeared somewhat mixed and unorganized.

Hierarchical Clustering Results: The positioning of the data points for hierarchical clustering was identical to the K-Means plot because the underlying data and scaling were the same. 
Overall Conclusion: The coloring and cluster grouping were much easier to visualize and looked significantly more organized. It clearly highlighted the inverse relationship between screen time and sleep duration with cleaner boundaries.

Principal Component Analysis Results: PC1: 55.2%, PC2: 33.3%, Total Variance Captured: 90.5%
Plot and Overall Conclusion: The PCA plot successfully compressed all features and groups onto a 2D graph. The plot displays a bunch horizontal lines (which is it likely grouping student ages or specific increments of time spent on social media). It captures 90.5% of the total variance which proves that even though we reduced our features from three dimensions down to two principal components, we successfully kept the most useful and important information from the dataset.
